    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d816">

                <pb facs="1279" n="1265" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg1265"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00116 European War 1939/1238: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-01-07T19:00:00+01:00">January 7, 1944—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 9:21 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>163. The wisdom of your message 8230 of December 30<anchor n="2" xml:id="d816-f1"
                    /> was apparent before it arrived. A press report had been released on Russian
                    participation which Mr. Pell<anchor n="3" xml:id="d816-f2"/> tells me had
                    presumably come from some member of the Commission talking out of turn. Mr. Pell
                    called the news item to the attention of the Chairman. I believe that there will
                    be less leakage at future meetings. I don’t think any harm was done.</p>
                <p>The question of Russian conditional membership has not been formally raised at a
                    meeting of the Commission. Mr. Eden,<anchor n="4" xml:id="d816-f3"/> Sir Cecil
                        Hurst<anchor n="5" xml:id="d816-f4"/> and Mr. Pell all agree that the
                    question of membership is the concern of governments and not of the
                    Commission.</p>
                <p>In discussing this point with Mr. Eden, he told me that his suggestion to the
                    Russians of having a single representative with each republic having an advisor
                    was not acceptable to them at the time he raised it. We might be in a stronger
                    position to press this point with our 48 States. The Russians already know the
                    British position. The British supporting Dominion representation makes plausible
                    the Russian position.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
